## Stabilization report

[RFC 3106](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3016-const-ub.html) permits undefined behavior in const evaluation, with the summary being the following:

> Define UB during const evaluation to lead to an unspecified result or hard error for the affected CTFE query, but not otherwise infect the compilation process.

This pull request stabilizes the `const_fn_transmute` and `const_fn_union` feature gates. Both `transmute` and `union` are presently stable in `const` and `static` statements, but not in `const fn`. This PR is the first to invoke the aforementioned RFC to my knowledge. These features are relied upon in the test suite, though strictly speaking this is somewhat unnecessary (as behavior when UB is present is by definition not defined). Some cases throw a compile-time error while others will succeed and have undefined behavior.

TLDR the following is now permitted:

```rust
const fn alpha(x: u32) -> (u16, u16) {
    unsafe { core::mem::transmute(x) }
}

union Beta {
    a: u32,
    b: (u16, u16),
}

// This is effectively a transmute, but that's not the point.
const fn beta(x: u32) -> (u16, u16) {
    unsafe { Beta { a: x }.b }
}
```

### Pointer-integer-transmutes

One particularly interesting case arises when transmuting a pointer to an integer (either via `transmute`, or via `union` field access): this operation is considered UB when it arises in const-context. The `transmute` documentation has been updated accordingly; we might want to also update other documentation elsewhere (suggestions welcome).

However, this will still let us write code like
```rust
pub const unsafe fn random() -> usize {
    let a = 5;
    std::mem::transmute(&a)
}
```
which would be the first stable `const fn` that is not guaranteed to behave deterministically at run-time.
This function certainly has UB when called at compile-time, but whether it has UB when called at run-time [is unclear](https://github.com/rust-lang/unsafe-code-guidelines/issues/286).

Here's an interesting example by @carbotaniuman for what this will let us do on stable:
```rust
pub const unsafe fn random() -> usize {
    let a = 5;
    std::mem::transmute(&a)
}
```
So, there is a relation here to the guarantee that a `const fn` will always behave the same even when called at run-time. The above code has UB during const-evaluation per the docs of this PR, but it is much less clear if it has UB at runtime (transmuting pointers to integers is incompatible with some optimizations so LLVM might have to disallow it).

On the other hand, if we have to lose one of "`transmute` in `cost fn`" and "all `const fn` are deterministic", then I think we probably rather want to lose the latter... and I see no good way to have our cake and eat it, too, here. Sorry Centril.

EDIT: incorporated into stabilization report.

